Let me explain the pieces the way I would when adding resilience to a flaky step in a scheduled job.

First, enable the feature with `@EnableRetry` on a configuration or the application class. Spring Retry works through AOP proxies, so the annotated method must be called through the proxy (a call from another bean, not a self-invocation within the same class), just like `@Transactional`.

`@Retryable` wraps the method. The core attributes are `retryFor` (which exceptions trigger a retry), `maxAttempts` (total attempts INCLUDING the first — so `maxAttempts = 3` means one initial try plus two retries), and `backoff`.

```java
@Retryable(
    retryFor = { TransientDataAccessException.class, ResourceAccessException.class },
    noRetryFor = { IllegalArgumentException.class },   // permanent errors fail fast
    maxAttempts = 4,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 500, multiplier = 2, maxDelay = 10_000))
public void syncRates() { /* calls a flaky external rate service */ }
```

`@Backoff` controls the spacing between attempts. `delay` is the initial wait; `multiplier` makes it exponential (500ms, then 1000ms, then 2000ms...); `maxDelay` caps the growth; and a `random` flag adds jitter to avoid a thundering herd when many instances retry together. Exponential backoff is the sensible default when retrying a shared dependency, because it gives the struggling service room to recover rather than pounding it.

`retryFor` versus `noRetryFor` encodes the crucial distinction between TRANSIENT and PERMANENT failures. A transient failure (a timeout, a brief connection reset, a deadlock) may succeed on retry, so I list it in `retryFor`. A permanent failure (invalid input, a business-rule violation, a 400 from an API) will NEVER succeed on retry, so retrying just wastes time and delays the inevitable — I put it in `noRetryFor` so it fails fast. Blindly retrying everything is an anti-pattern.

`@Recover` is the fallback invoked after all attempts are exhausted. Its signature rules matter: the FIRST parameter must be the throwable (matching the exception that exhausted retries), and the remaining parameters must match the original method's arguments; the return type must match the retryable method. Spring picks the recover method whose exception type most closely matches.

```java
@Recover
public void recoverSyncRates(TransientDataAccessException ex) {
    // all retries failed → log, alert, write to a dead-letter / failure table, do NOT rethrow
    log.error("Rate sync failed after retries", ex);
    failureStore.record("rate-sync", ex.getMessage());
}
```

A few senior nuances. First, in a scheduled job I apply `@Retryable` to the UNIT OF WORK (a single chunk or a single external call), not to the whole multi-hour job, so one transient blip retries cheaply without re-running everything. Second, retry and transactions interact carefully: if the retryable method is `@Transactional`, each retry should be its own transaction (the failed attempt's transaction must roll back before the next attempt), so I structure the retry around a method that starts a fresh transaction per attempt. Third, retried operations should be IDEMPOTENT, because an attempt might have partially succeeded before failing. Fourth, for distributed or cross-service retries I prefer a durable mechanism (a message queue with redelivery, or Spring Batch's retry/skip policies) over in-memory `@Retryable`, because an in-memory retry is lost if the process dies. Fifth, the programmatic `RetryTemplate` is available when I need retry logic that cannot be expressed declaratively.

